TTYCHATTER(1)               User Commands               TTYCHATTER(1)

NAME
    ttychatter - OpenRouter terminal chat client, C command-line edition

SYNOPSIS
    ttychatter [options] input.txt output.log
    ttychatter --interactive [SESSION|./output.log]
    ttychatter --resume SESSION
    ttychatter --demo [options] input.txt output.log
    ttychatter --models [--search TEXT] [--model-type TYPE] [--sort KEY]
    ttychatter --routers
    ttychatter --select-model
    ttychatter --test-model MODEL [--save]
    ttychatter --set-api-key [--gpg]
    ttychatter --forget-api-key
    ttychatter --list
    ttychatter --rename-session OLD NEW
    ttychatter --show-memory SESSION
    ttychatter --edit-memory SESSION
    ttychatter --clear-memory SESSION
    ttychatter --editor-prompt [output.log]
    ttychatter --search TEXT [--all-sessions]
    ttychatter --config
    ttychatter --set KEY=VALUE
    ttychatter --unset KEY
    ttychatter --doctor
    ttychatter --version
    ttychatter --help

DESCRIPTION
    ttychatter is the traditional Unix command-line C client in the
    ttychatter project.  It is not an ncurses program and does not own the
    full terminal screen.  Its primary interface is file-in/file-out:

        ttychatter input.txt output.log

    The user creates input.txt using any editor or shell tool.  ttychatter
    sends that input to OpenRouter, appends the result to output.log, and
    later uses the same output.log as session history/context when the same
    path is reused.

    The C client also has a line-oriented interactive mode with local colon
    commands.  Interactive mode is intentionally still plain line I/O, not a
    curses UI.

SESSION ADDRESSING
    ttychatter supports two address modes.

    Explicit-path mode is used by the traditional two-file command:

        ttychatter input.txt output.log
        ttychatter followup.txt output.log

    In this form, output.log is used exactly as written.  It may be a
    relative path, an absolute path, or a path under any project directory.
    Reusing the same output path resumes that same conversation.

    Named-session mode is used by --interactive, --resume, --list,
    --rename-session, --show-memory, --edit-memory, --clear-memory, and
    --search-sessions.  A bare SESSION name resolves under SESSION_DIR.  With
    the default OpenRouter storage, these are equivalent:

        ttychatter --resume notes
        ttychatter --resume notes.log
        ~/.local/share/ttychatter/openrouter/sessions/notes.log

    A SESSION containing '/' is treated as an explicit path.  Use ./notes.log
    when the intended file is in the current directory rather than SESSION_DIR.

USAGE EXAMPLES
    Save an API key in plaintext config:

        ttychatter --set-api-key

    Save an API key encrypted with gpg:

        ttychatter --set-api-key --gpg

    Refresh the model cache:

        ttychatter --update-models

    List cached router models:

        ttychatter --models --model-type routers
        ttychatter --routers

    Search cached models:

        ttychatter --models --search llama

    Select a model from a numbered cached list:

        ttychatter --select-model

    Test one model without saving it:

        ttychatter --test-model openrouter/auto

    Test one model and save it as MODEL on success:

        ttychatter --test-model openrouter/auto --save

    Send an input file and append to an explicit session file:

        ttychatter prompt.txt session.log

    Resume that explicit session file:

        ttychatter followup.txt session.log

    Start a named interactive session in the shared session directory:

        ttychatter --interactive notes

    Resume a named interactive session:

        ttychatter --resume notes

    Attach multiple files:

        ttychatter -a notes.txt -a diagram.png prompt.txt session.log

    Print the AI response to stdout for shell piping:

        ttychatter --loopback prompt.txt session.log | sed -n '1,20p'

RUNTIME COMMANDS
    In interactive mode, lines beginning with ':' are local ttychatter
    commands.  They are not sent to the AI model.

        :help
        :list
        :rename NAME
        :models
        :routers
        :update-models
        :select-model
        :test-model MODEL
        :model MODEL
        :model-save MODEL
        :favorites
        :favorite MODEL
        :unfavorite MODEL
        :unbookmark MODEL
        :config
        :set KEY VALUE
        :unset KEY
        :set-api-key
        :set-api-key gpg
        :forget-api-key
        :memory
        :edit-memory
        :clear-memory
        :attach FILE
        :attachments
        :pending
        :clear-attachments
        :editor
        :search TEXT
        :search-all TEXT
        :credits
        :doctor
        :quit

    To send a literal message beginning with a colon, prefix it with a
    backslash:

        \:this line is sent to the AI

MODEL MANAGEMENT
    ttychatter uses OpenRouter's model catalog.  The model list is cached
    locally so ordinary browsing does not require network access.

        ttychatter --update-models
        ttychatter --models
        ttychatter --models --search TEXT
        ttychatter --models --model-type routers
        ttychatter --models --favorites-only
        ttychatter --models --sort context

    Supported model type filters:

        all
        routers
        fixed
        free
        auto

    Supported sort keys:

        name
        context
        tokens
        type
        favorites

    Related filters:

        --min-input-tokens N
        --min-output-tokens N
        --hide-preview
        --require-tokens
        --allow-missing-tokens
        --all

FAVORITES
    Favorite models are stored as plain text, one model per line:

        ttychatter --favorite-model openrouter/auto
        ttychatter --unfavorite-model openrouter/auto
        ttychatter --favorites

API KEY STORAGE
    ttychatter checks for API keys in this order:

        OPENROUTER_API_KEY environment variable
        TTYCHATTER_API_KEY environment variable
        OPENROUTER_API_KEY, TTYCHATTER_API_KEY, or API_KEY in config
        API_KEY_GPG_FILE via external gpg decryption
        legacy C default gpg file as a fallback

    GPG storage uses the external gpg program.  No cryptographic code is
    embedded in ttychatter itself.  The decrypted key is not printed by
    --config or --doctor.

CONFIGURATION
    Default OpenRouter config file:

        ~/.config/ttychatter/openrouter/config

    If that file does not exist, ttychatter also reads the legacy C config:

        ~/.config/ttychatter/config

    This is a read-only compatibility fallback.  New writes go to the
    OpenRouter config path.

    Important keys include:

        OPENROUTER_API_KEY
        TTYCHATTER_API_KEY
        API_KEY
        API_KEY_GPG_FILE
        MODEL
        MODEL_CACHE_FILE
        MODEL_FAVORITES_FILE
        MODEL_TYPE_FILTER
        MODEL_SORT_ORDER
        MODEL_FILTER_GENERATE_CONTENT
        MODEL_FILTER_CHAT_COMPLETIONS
        MODEL_FILTER_REQUIRE_TOKENS
        MODEL_FILTER_HIDE_PREVIEW
        MODEL_MIN_INPUT_TOKENS
        MODEL_MIN_OUTPUT_TOKENS
        MODEL_TEST_PROMPT
        CONTEXT_TURNS
        HISTORY_LIMIT
        SESSION_DIR
        ATTACHMENT_DIR
        CODE_ATTACHMENT_MIN_LINES
        MAX_ATTACHMENT_BYTES
        CONFIRM_LIVE_SEND
        STARTUP_NOTICE
        DEMO_MODE
        LOOPBACK
        SEND_INPUT
        THEME
        EDITOR

    Some keys, such as SEND_INPUT and THEME, exist mostly for cross-client
    config parity.  The file-in/file-out C command does not have an input key
    binding or terminal theme in the same sense as the ncurses client.

SESSION FORMAT
    New logs start with this pre-chat line:

        Everyone is encouraged to get the cruelty-free vegetarian alternatives and remember the bloodguilt curse from the Bible... http://bloodguiltcurse.net

    New C logs then use the shared OpenRouter ttychatter line-oriented
    transcript format:

        [HH:MM:SS] User: ...
        [HH:MM:SS] AI: ...
        [HH:MM:SS] system: CONTEXT_BEGIN
        User: ...
        Assistant: ...
        [HH:MM:SS] system: CONTEXT_END

    This keeps sessions readable and compatible with the other OpenRouter
    ttychatter clients.  The C parser also reads the older 0.6.0 C marker
    format so existing C session logs remain resumable.

ATTACHMENTS
    Attach files with -a or --attach.  Text-like files are inserted as text
    parts.  Some media files may be sent as data URLs when model/provider
    support allows it.

    Long fenced code blocks in responses are extracted into ATTACHMENT_DIR.
    Short code blocks remain inline.  The line threshold is controlled by
    CODE_ATTACHMENT_MIN_LINES.

DEMO MODE
    --demo uses local demo models and local demo responses.  It does not
    require an API key and does not contact OpenRouter.  This is useful for
    testing logs, attachments, loopback, and the interactive command loop.

FILES
    ~/.config/ttychatter/openrouter/config
        Shared OpenRouter ttychatter configuration file.

    ~/.config/ttychatter/openrouter/api-key.gpg
        Default encrypted OpenRouter API key file.

    ~/.config/ttychatter/openrouter/model-favorites
        Shared plain-text favorite model list.

    ~/.cache/ttychatter/openrouter/models.json
        Cached OpenRouter model catalog.

    ~/.local/share/ttychatter/openrouter/sessions/
        Default directory for named sessions.

    ~/.local/share/ttychatter/openrouter/attachments/
        Default attachment and extracted-code directory.

    ~/.config/ttychatter/config
        Legacy C config fallback, read only when the OpenRouter config file is
        absent.

VERSION
    ttychatter 0.6.1-c-cli

AUTHOR
    Part of the ttychatter terminal AI client project.

PARITY NOTES
    The C client keeps its Unix file-in/file-out interface, but its default
    config, API-key, model-cache, favorites, attachment, and named-session
    storage now live in the same OpenRouter namespace as the other OpenRouter
    ttychatter clients.  Interface independence is preserved; storage
    independence is not the default.
